    "answer": {
      "short": "$g(x)=x+1$, with domain $\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{-1\\}$. The original function is undefined at $x=-1$, so its reciprocal is also undefined there.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}g(x)&=\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{x+1}}\\\\g(x)&=x+1\\qquad(x\\ne-1).\\end{align*}$$\nThe reciprocal is defined only where $f(x)$ is defined and nonzero. Here $f(x)$ is undefined at $x=-1$ and is never zero. Simplifying the expression does not restore the excluded value, so the domain is $\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{-1\\}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 22
